      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s5",
      "source_id": "aa1ab28d-e35b-48b6-a235-db000f20a65b",
      "text": "That's a common dilemma! You want high reliability without over-engineering the system, which usually means a \"lean, but resilient\" approach.\n\nHere is a strategy focused on minimizing complexity while maximizing survival:\n\n### 1. The \"Minimum Viable System\" (MVS) Approach\n*   **The \"Core\" Function:** If the primary goal is data collection, focus your \"must-have\" component—likely the data logger/microcontroller—and ensure *that* specific piece has a robust, small, dedicated battery/power source.\n*   **The Redundancy Rule:** Instead of making everything redundant (which adds complexity), only make the **most critical component** redundant. \n    *   **Example:** If the main data logger/controller fails, do you have a backup \"last-ditch\" controller/device? If that fails, the whole mission stops. If not, can the system switch to a manual logging mode?\n\n### 2. Practical Implementation for Low-Supply Areas:\n*   **If you have \"infrequent deliveries,\" your best bet is a \"Buffer/Cache\" system:**\n    *   **Data Buffer:** Make sure your main storage is robust enough to hold data for **"
